<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Otto I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">crowned <a href="/years/962">962 CE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Otto I of Saxony · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottonian-dynasty">Ottonian dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-the-illustrious">Otto I, Duke of Saxony</a> and King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, was crowned emperor by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-john-xii">Pope John XII</a> in 962 CE. He had earlier defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyars</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lechfeld">Battle of Lechfeld</a> in 955. His coronation is the date most historians treat as the functional founding of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/otto-i.jpg" alt="Otto I" width="320" height="310" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Otto Posse · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Siegel_Otto_I_Posse.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Otto was born on 23 November 912, the eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-fowler">Henry the Fowler</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-of-saxony">Duke of Saxony</a>, and his second wife Matilda, daughter of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> count Dietrich of Ringelheim. In May 919, at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-diet">Imperial Diet</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fritzlar">Fritzlar</a>, Frankish and Saxon nobles elected Henry king, so that for the first time a Saxon reigned over the kingdom. Otto gained military experience in campaigns against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wends">Wends</a> on the eastern border, and by about 929 his father was probably preparing the succession. Henry died on 2 July 936, when all the German tribes were united in a single realm.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Otto was crowned on 7 August 936 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen">Aachen</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>'s former capital, by Hildebert, Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a>. Though a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a>, he wore Frankish dress to assert his authority over Lotharingia and his role as Charlemagne's true successor. He put down rebellions by dukes, among them Eberhard of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, whom he exiled in 938, and he installed members of his own family in the great duchies. Loyalty to Otto, not lineage, became the way to advancement. He also made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> and abbots his instruments of government, granting them land and the title of Prince of the Empire; because these offices fell back to the crown at death, hereditary claims were avoided.</p><p>Otto's son Liudolf, Duke of Swabia, and his son-in-law Conrad, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a>, rebelled in 951, resenting Otto's marriage to Adelaide, Queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, and the power of his brother Henry. During the civil war the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyars</a> ravaged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconia">Franconia</a>. Otto defeated them at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lechfeld">Battle of Lechfeld</a> in 955, which ended the Hungarian invasions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a> and earned him a reputation as a saviour of Christendom.</p><p>By 961 Otto had conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a>. On 12 February 962, as emperor, he and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-john-xii">Pope John XII</a> held a synod in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, which approved his long-desired Archdiocese of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a>. Relations with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> soon soured: in 964 Otto besieged Rome, forcing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> to accept his appointee Leo VIII as pope. Seeking peace with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>, which opposed his claim to emperorship, he arranged the marriage of his son Otto II to the princess Theophanu in April 972.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Otto returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in August 972 and died at Memleben on 7 May 973; Otto II succeeded him. His patronage helped create several cathedral schools and the Ottonian Renaissance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> and architecture. Hrotsvitha, a poet and dramatist, was raised at his court. Historians have long depicted him as a successful ruler and a gifted commander, though the realm he built was too vast for the administrative structures of the time and could only be governed as a confederacy.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Otto the Great” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 936 Abd al-Rahman III began building the palace city of Madinat al-Zahra west of Córdoba, where foreign envoys, among them embassies from Byzantium and from <b>Otto I</b> of Germany, were received with elaborate ritual.</blockquote><a href="/empire/caliphate-of-cordoba">Caliphate of Córdoba · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In 959 Archbishop Bruno of Cologne, brother of <b>Otto I</b> and duke of Lotharingia, divided it into Lower Lorraine in the north and Upper Lorraine along the Moselle and upper Meuse.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-lorraine">Duchy of Lorraine · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Under <b>Otto I</b> the Saxon frontier became a system of marches: Hermann Billung commanded on the lower Elbe and Margrave Gero in the middle Elbe lands, imposing tribute on the Slavs.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-saxony">Duchy of Saxony · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After the fragmentation of the Carolingian realm the imperial succession lapsed and was contested until <b>Otto I of Saxony</b>, having defeated the Magyars at the Battle of Lechfeld in 955 CE, was crowned emperor by Pope John XII in 962 CE, the date most historians treat as the…</blockquote><a href="/empire/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Duchy of Saxony, ruled by the Liudolfing family, produced the kings of East Francia from 919, beginning with Henry the Fowler, and his son <b>Otto I</b> was crowned emperor in 962.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saxons">Saxons · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Holy Roman Emperor <b>Otto I</b> conferred the royal dignity and title on him after his death, which is why the legend and the song call him a king.</blockquote><a href="/songs/good-king-wenceslas">Good King Wenceslas</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Wallhausen, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Memleben, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.47,11.18&amp;year=962" aria-label="Wallhausen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Otto I"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5592 5677h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5592 5677h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.47,11.18&amp;year=962">What was here in 962 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
